  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 345 AM CDT Fri Oct 16 2026\n\nCurrent surface analysis places low over the eastern Great Lakes\nregion while high settles through the eastern Montana/Wyoming\nand the western Dakotas. Upper level analysis places trough over\nthe upper Midwest, while ridge stretches from the northern\nSierra northward into the Canadian Rockies. Over our area, winds\ncontinue to gradually diminish from west to east with the high\nsliding through. In addition, where winds have tapered off and\nskies have cleared over our northern/western areas, temperatures \nhave dipped below freezing, thus the Freeze Warning remains in \nplaces.\n\nFor today, surface high settles southeast with return flow\nsetting up over western parts of our area, with southerly winds\nincreasing. Upper ridge pushes east as a notable wave over the\nnorthern Pacific approaches shore. This will bring clearing\nskies and dry conditions to our area. Over far southwest North\nDakota, humidity values are expected to dip into the 20s, which\ncombined with the expected southerly winds will result in near\ncritical fire weather conditions.\n\nOn Saturday, upper ridge pushes over our area while\naforementioned Pacific wave quickly passes over the Canadian\nRockies pushing into the western Canadian Prairies late day.\nSurface low closes off over northern Alberta/Saskatchewan in\nresponse, with winds shifting southwesterly over our area. \nTemperatures will climb to about 10 to 20 degrees above average,\nwarmest over the southwest where highs in the mid 70s are \nexpected, with mainly lower 60s to lower 70s elsewhere. \n\nUpper wave continues its eastward push flattening out the ridge,\nwhile a modest cold front passes through our area as surface low\nworks its way towards Hudson Bay late Saturday night into early\nSunday. Though conditions remain dry, temperatures will be\ncooler with highs in the mid 50s to mid 60s. \n\nZonal flow remains in place to start the work week, with flow\nturning southwesterly towards mid-week as a Pacific low drops\ndown along the West Coast. Conditions remain dry, with a very gradual\nwarming trend over our area. Guidance then suggests that the low\nshifts easterly and opens up late week. Though the timing of\nthis is a bit variable, it does bring precipitation chances to\nthe forecast late week.",
